[1mzluks[0m

  [1mSYNOPSIS[0m

    [1mzluks[0m [[1munlock[0m]

    [1mzluks[0m [1mopen[0m [33mdevice[0m [[33mname[0m]

    [1mzluks[0m [1mclose[0m [[33mname[0m]

    [1mzluks[0m [1mstatus[0m

    [1mzluks[0m [1mlist[0m

    [1mzluks[0m [1mhelp[0m

  [1mDESCRIPTION[0m

    [1mzluks[0m manages LUKS (dm-crypt) volumes that hold
    ZFS vdevs from within a running ZFSBootMenu
    image: from the recovery shell, or from an
    SSH session when remote access is
    configured. It uses the same unlock
    procedure that ZFSBootMenu runs at start-up,
    described in zfsbootmenu-luks(7), so key
    scripts, key files, GnuPG-encrypted key
    files, LUKS2 tokens (TPM2, FIDO2 and
    clevis), YubiKey challenge-response and
    passphrases all work the same way.

    Mappings opened by [1mzluks[0m or by ZFSBootMenu
    itself are recorded, so that they are closed
    again before ZFSBootMenu boots a boot
    environment. Mappings opened directly with
    [1mcryptsetup[0m(8) are not recorded and are left
    alone.

    After unlocking volumes, type [1mexit[0m in the
    recovery shell to return to ZFSBootMenu,
    which retries the pool import, or import
    pools manually with [1mzpool import[0m.

    [1mzluks[0m is only present in images built with LUKS
    support (the default when [1mcryptsetup[0m is
    installed on the build host).

  [1mSUBCOMMANDS[0m

    [1munlock[0m

      Unlock every LUKS volume selected by the
      configuration, exactly as ZFSBootMenu
      does at start-up: the volumes listed in
      [1mzbm.luks[0m, otherwise those in the image's
      crypttab, otherwise every LUKS volume on
      the system. Volumes that are already
      open are skipped. This is the default
      when no subcommand is given.

    [1mopen[0m [33mdevice[0m [[33mname[0m]

      Unlock a single LUKS volume. [33mdevice[0m is a path
      below [33m/dev[0m or a device specification of
      the form [1mUUID=[0m, [1mPARTUUID=[0m, [1mLABEL=[0m,
      [1mPARTLABEL=[0m, [1mID=[0m ([33m/dev/disk/by-id[0m) or
      [1mPATH=[0m ([33m/dev/disk/by-path[0m). A bare LUKS
      UUID, or [1mluks-[0m[33mUUID[0m, is also accepted.

      The mapping is created as [33m/dev/mapper/[0m[33mname[0m.
      When [33mname[0m is omitted, the mapping is
      called [1mluks-[0m[33mUUID[0m, which is also the
      default name used by systemd-cryptsetup
      and dracut. The key file given by
      [1mzbm.luks_key[0m, if any, is tried before
      the volume's LUKS2 tokens and a
      passphrase prompt.

    [1mclose[0m [[33mname[0m]

      Export every imported pool that has a vdev on
      a mapping opened by ZFSBootMenu, then
      close the mapping. Data, special, log,
      cache and spare vdevs are recognized,
      including vdevs on partitions inside a
      mapping. With [33mname[0m, only that mapping is
      closed. A mapping whose pools cannot be
      exported stays open and is reported.

    [1mstatus[0m

      List the mappings opened by ZFSBootMenu:
      mapping name, LUKS device, whether the
      mapping is open, and the imported pools
      that use it.

    [1mlist[0m

      List every LUKS volume on the system with its
      size and label, whether it is in use
      (opened by anything), and the types of
      LUKS2 tokens in its header
      ([1msystemd-tpm2[0m, [1msystemd-fido2[0m, [1mclevis[0m or
      others).

    [1mhelp[0m, [1m-h[0m, [1m--help[0m

      Print a usage summary.

  [1mENVIRONMENT VARIABLES[0m

    [1mLUKS_TPM2_TCTI[0m

      The TPM2 software stack TCTI used for TPM2 and
      clevis tokens. The default is
      [33mdevice:/dev/tpmrm0[0m.

    [1mLUKS_NO_TOKEN_PLUGINS[0m

      When set to any non-empty value, ignore
      systemd's cryptsetup token plugins and
      use ZFSBootMenu's own TPM2 and FIDO2
      implementations. Useful for diagnosing
      plugin problems.

    [1mLUKS_DEFAULT_TRIES[0m

      Passphrase attempts per round when the
      crypttab sets no [1mtries=[0m option. The
      default is 3.

    [1mZBM_CRYPTTAB[0m

      The crypttab to read. The default is
      [33m/etc/zfsbootmenu/crypttab[0m.

  [1mFILES[0m

    [33m/etc/zfsbootmenu/crypttab[0m

      Volumes to unlock, embedded in the image at
      build time; see zfsbootmenu-crypttab(5).

    [33m/etc/zfsbootmenu/gnupg/[0m

      Public keyring used to decrypt GnuPG-encrypted
      key files.

    [33m/zfsbootmenu/luks-mappings[0m

      Mappings opened by ZFSBootMenu: name, device
      and whether the volume was discovered
      automatically.

    [33m/zfsbootmenu/luks-skipped[0m

      Volumes the user chose to skip; they are not
      offered again when ZFSBootMenu retries
      the pool import.

  [1mEXIT STATUS[0m

    [1m0[0m

      Successful completion. For [1munlock[0m, every
      selected volume was opened, or at least
      one was opened.

    [1m1[0m

      An error occurred: a volume could not be
      unlocked, a device was not found, a
      mapping could not be closed, [1mcryptsetup[0m
      is not available, or the command line
      was invalid.

  [1mEXAMPLES[0m

    [1mExample 1: Listing LUKS volumes and their tokens[0m

      The following command shows every LUKS volume
      that ZFSBootMenu can see. The first two
      volumes hold a mirror and are locked;
      the third has a TPM2 token and is
      already open:

      # zluks list
      /dev/sda2 "zfs0" (931.5G)   locked   tokens: systemd-fido2
      /dev/sdb2 "zfs1" (931.5G)   locked   tokens: systemd-fido2
      /dev/nvme0n1p2 (476.9G)     in use   tokens: systemd-tpm2

    [1mExample 2: Unlocking all volumes from the recovery shell[0m

      After skipping the passphrase prompt at
      start-up, unlock the configured volumes
      and return to ZFSBootMenu, which imports
      the pools:

      # zluks
      Unlock LUKS volume /dev/sda2 "zfs0" (931.5G)
      Passphrase (leave empty to skip):
      # exit

    [1mExample 3: Unlocking one volume with a chosen mapping name[0m

      Hot spare and cache vdevs are located by the
      device path recorded in the pool. To
      make such a vdev available, open its
      LUKS volume under the same name the
      running system uses:

      # zluks open PARTLABEL=spare0 luks-3f1c6e0a-5b7d-4e8b-9d3e-2a6c1f0e9b42
      # zpool status zroot

    [1mExample 4: Unlocking over SSH while the console waits[0m

      When ZFSBootMenu waits at a passphrase prompt
      on the console, an SSH session can
      unlock the same volume; the console
      prompt notices and continues:

      $ ssh -p 222 root@zbm-host
      # zluks open UUID=0a1b2c3d-4e5f-6789-abcd-ef0123456789

    [1mExample 5: Showing and closing mappings[0m

      The following commands show which pools use
      the mappings opened by ZFSBootMenu, then
      export the pool [33mtank[0m and close its
      mapping:

      # zluks status
      luks-0a1b2c3d-...   /dev/sdc2   open   pools: tank
      luks-9f8e7d6c-...   /dev/sdd2   open   pools: zroot
      # zluks close luks-0a1b2c3d-4e5f-6789-abcd-ef0123456789

  [1mSEE ALSO[0m

    zfsbootmenu-luks(7), zfsbootmenu-crypttab(5),
    zfsbootmenu(7), [1mcryptsetup[0m(8),
    [1msystemd-cryptenroll[0m(1), [1mzpool[0m(8)
